   "text": "ruminating. animals frona their lst stomach, and chewed a 2d time; a piece of chewing tobacco; quid.Cuddle, kud'dl, v. i. [cUDDLED (-dld), -DLING.]To lie close or snug ; to squat, crouch, snuggle.—Cud'dy, -dY, n. (Naut.) À small cabin in a boat. A very small apartment. Cudgel, kuj’el, n.A short, thick stick; club.—v. t.[cuDGELED (-eld), -ELING.]. To beat.Cue, ku, n. A tail ; esp. a tail-like twist of hair at the back of the head; last words of an aetor's speech,regarded as a hint for the succeeding player to speak; hint or iatimation; the part one is to perform;a straight rod used in playing billiards.Cuff, kuf, n. A blow with the open hand ;a stroke ;box; buffet.—v. t.[CUFFED (kuft), CUFFING.] To strike with the flat of the hand, as a man ; or with talons or wings, as a fowl.—n. The fold at the end of a sleeve. Cuirass, kwe-ras', n. A piece of armor, covering the body from neck to girdle. —Cuirassier, -sēr', n. A soldier armed with, ete. [F.J Cuish, kwis,n.Defensive armor for the thighs.Cuisine, kwe-zēn', n. The kitchen : style of cooking ;eookery.—Cu'linary, -na-rI, a. Pert. to the kitchen or cookery. Cul-de-gac, koold'sak',n, A street elosed at one end;a trap. (Mil.) A position in which an army has no exit but to the front. (Nat. Hist.) A bag-shaped eavity, or organ, open only at one end.Cull, kul, v. t.°[eULLED (kuld), cULLING.To separate, select, or pick out. Cullender. See CoLANDER. Culminate, kul'mi-nāt, v. i.To reach the highest point of altitude, or of rank, size, numbers, ete.a. Growing upward, as disting. fr. lateral growth.—Culmina'tion, n.Attainment of, ete. ; passage across the meridian; transit. Éulpable, kul/pa-bl, a. Deserving censure; worthy of blame; faulty; censurable.—Culpableness, -bil'ity, -tí, n.—CuÍ'pably, alv.— Cul'prit, n. One accused or convieted of erime ; a eriminal.Cultus, kul'tus, Cult, n. Homage; worship: a system of religious, belief, worship, or rites. —Cul'tivate,0.t.To till, fertilize ; to direct special attention to, foster, cherish ; to improve by labor, care, or study : to civilize, refine.—Cul'tivable, a.— Cultiva'tion, n. Art or practice of,ete. ; tillage ; fostering eare ; civilization: state of being eultivated ;advancement in physical, intellectual, or moral con dition: refinement ; culture. —Cul'tivator, -tēr, n. One w ho, ete. ; an innplement used in the tillage of growing erops, to loosen the surface of the earth.— Culture,kul'chur, n. Act of, ete. eultivation ; refinement of Cultivator.mind or manners. —v. t. [cULTURED (-churd), -TURING.To eultivate. Culter,, kul'tér, n. A colter.See CoLTER.—Cul'.trate, -trated, a. (Bot. & Ornith.) Sharp-edged and pointed, like a pruning knife. Culvert, kul'vērt, n. An arehed drain for water under a road, canal, ete. Cumber, kum’bēr, v. t.. [-BERED (-bērd), -BERING.]To hang or rest on as a troublesome weight; to be burdensome.or oppressive to; to clog, obstruct, embarrass, impede.Cumbersome, -sum, a. Burdensome or hindering ; not easily managed: oppressive;vexatious.—Cum'bersomely, adu.Cum'brance,-brans,n.Eneumbrance.— Cum'brous, -brus, a.Rendering action difficult; giving trouble.—Cum.brously,adv. —Cum'brousness,n.—Cu'mulate,-lat,v.t.To heap together; amass.-Cumula'tion,n. Act of, ete.; a heap.—Cu'mulative, -tiv, a. Forming a mass; aggregated; augmenting: gaining or giving force by successive additions. (Law.) Given by the same testator to the same legatee, — said of a primary forms of elouds,—being massed, and often bringing rain. [L.] — Cu'mulo-stra'tus, n.of cloud between eumulus and stratus, which is in layers. Cumin, kum’in, n., An umbelliferous plant, resembling fennel, and having afomatic seeds.",
